 programme of exhibitions, lectures, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Academic_conference\">symposia</a> and publications have given it a central position in global discussions and developments within contemporary architectural culture.","subtitle":"Architectural Association School of Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/aa.jpg","external":"http://www.aaschool.ac.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/36+Bedford+Square,+AA+School+of+Architecture,+Fitzrovia,+London+WC1B+3ES,+UK/@51.5183066,-0.1307022,17z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":702,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":1172.2,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":195.92,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":388.16,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":483.92,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":636.36,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":3.32,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":527.72,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":657.92,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":764.56,"topicId":"technology","topicLabel":"Technology"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":453.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":641.96,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1068.04,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1965.44,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":295.64,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":636.52,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":884.76,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":266.96,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":350.88,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":575,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":738.8,"topicId":"appearance","topicLabel":"Appearance"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":498.32,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":518.88,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"Alison and Peter Smithson":{"slug":"alison-and-peter-smithson","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England\">English</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architects</a> Alison Smithson (22 June 1928 – 16 August 1993) and Peter Smithson (18 September 1923 – 3 March 2003) together formed an architectural partnership, and are often associated with the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Brutalism\">New Brutalism</a> (especially in architectural and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_theory\">urban theory</a>). Peter was born in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stockton-on-Tees\">Stockton-on-Tees</a> in north-east England, and Alison was born in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheffield\">Sheffield</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Yorkshire\">South Yorkshire</a>. They met while studying architecture at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durham_University\">Durham University</a> and married in 1949. Together, they joined the architecture department of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_County_Council\">London County Council</a> before establishing their own partnership in 1950.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1903.76,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"}]},"Blackpool Restaurant":{"slug":"blackpool-restaurant","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">A ninety-foot, clear-span, square, steel framed, mixed serviced restaurant with its own kitchen and stores due to its isolated location. Timber-clad and lined throughout with ply and steel mirrors, this is a cosy cigar-box of a building through whose tinted windows only those seated can watch the Zoo’s residents. (C.P. in The Square Book, p. 81)</p>","subtitle":"Cedric Price architects","image":null,"external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/903-an-interview-with-david-price-brother-and-model-maker-to","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/blackpool+zoo/@53.817109,-3.016465,15z","also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1771.92,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"}]},"British Airports Authority Office, Heathrow":{"slug":"british-airports-authority-office-heathrow","body":"Here on a site adjacent to one of the main runways at London Airport, the strict height controls and massive noise interference anabled one to utilise the unusable – in the form of a narrow, road-boudned strip of land. Roof-parking, helping the sound insulation, enabled multiple spot access to the very centre of this single-floor, open-plan office – the ideal access condiction for burolandschaft.  (CP in The Square Book, p. 43)","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":669.44,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"BTDB Computer Centre":{"slug":"btdb-computer-centre","body":"A lightweight building simply sitting on a reinforced concrete raft. The steel columns had to be sufficiently precise to take window gaskets.  (Frank Newby in The Square Book, p. 82)","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":677.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1763.6,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"}]},"Buckminster Fuller":{"slug":"buckminster-fuller","body":"Richard Buckminster \"Bucky\" Fuller published more than 30 books, coining or popularizing terms such as \"<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceship_Earth\">Spaceship Earth</a>\", <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephemeralization\">ephemeralization</a>, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synergetics_(Fuller)\">synergetic</a>. He also developed numerous inventions, mainly architectural designs, and popularized the widely known <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geodesic_dome\">geodesic dome</a>. Carbon molecules known as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fullerene\">fullerenes</a> were later named by scientists for their resemblance to geodesic spheres. Buckminster Fuller was the second president of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mensa_International\">Mensa</a> from 1974 to 1983.","subtitle":"Neo-futuristic architect, systems theorist, author, designer, and inventor.","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/buckminster-fuller.jpg","external":"https://bfi.org","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Milton,+MA,+USA/@42.2495321,-71.0661653,7z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":438.2,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1350.68,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":123.44,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":549.32,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1393.16,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"Cedric Price Office":{"slug":"cedric-price-office","body":"","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":444.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1751.88,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":43.4,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":1.64,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":314.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":442.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":184.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":247.32,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1065.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1063.84,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":272.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":181.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":466.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":26.88,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":716.28,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":743.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1639.24,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":2.64,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":568.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":672,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":761.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"Cybernetics":{"slug":"cybernetics","body":"Cybernetics is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transdisciplinarity\">transdisciplinary</a> approach for exploring regulatory <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systems\">systems</a>, their <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structure\">structures</a>, constraints, and possibilities. Cybernetics is relevant to the study of systems, such as mechanical, physical, biological, cognitive, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_systems\">social systems</a>.","subtitle":"κυβερνητική / government","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/cybernetics.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybernetics","location":null,"also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":668.8,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":896.24,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":21.84,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":711.28,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":85.56,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"David Allford":{"slug":"david-allford","body":"In an age when famous architects become superstars, it is easy to forget that media personalities are unreliable indicators of architectural abilities. David Allford was not a media personality - although a leading architectural journalist remembers him \"growling splendidly into the microphone\" in a recent radio broadcast when they were both panellists - yet most people in this country have probably experienced a building where his hand can be detected. Among them are Gatwick Airport, several large hospitals including St Thomas' in London and Hull Royal Infirmary, numerous comprehensive schools and offices, Warwick University and Cole Brothers department store in his home town of Sheffield.","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/david-allford.jpg","external":"http://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/obituary-david-allford-1246217.html","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":312.16,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1543.48,"topicId":"food-and-drink","topicLabel":"Food and drink"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":446.92,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1237.2,"topicId":"food-and-drink","topicLabel":"Food and drink"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":835.36,"topicId":"hot-stuff-club","topicLabel":"Hot Stuff Club"}]},"Frank Newby":{"slug":"frank-newby","body":"Frank Newby (26 March 1926 – 10 May 2001) was one of the leading structural engineers of the 20th century, working with such architects as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Powell_(architect)\">Philip Powell</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hidalgo_Moya\">Hidalgo Moya</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen\">Eero Saarinen</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Price\">Cedric Price</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Stirling_(architect)\">James Stirling</a>, and the practice of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skidmore,_Owings_and_Merrill\">Skidmore, Owings and Merrill</a> (SOM), and such engineers as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ove_Arup\">Ove Arup</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_Samuely\">Felix Samuely</a>.","subtitle":"Structural engineer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/frank-newby.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Newby","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1189.84,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":350,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":449.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":552.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1239.4,"topicId":"food-and-drink","topicLabel":"Food and drink"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":833.88,"topicId":"hot-stuff-club","topicLabel":"Hot Stuff Club"}]},"FUN PALACE":{"slug":"fun-palace","body":"Fun Palace is Cedric Price’s most celebrated work. Whether characterised as a giant toy or as a building-sized transformable machine, the project’s interest resides in its radical reliance on structure and technology, its exemplification of notions of time-based and anticipatory architecture. With Fun Palace, Price addressed social and political issues that go far beyond the typical bounds of architecture. Fun Palace was conceived and commissioned in 1961 by renowned theatre director and producer Joan Littlewood. Price developed plans for the project through 1964, both for the main project and for a smaller, more mobile “pilot” project. Neither were realised. Attempts to get planning permission for a wide variety of sites within and around London continued through 1970, amidst opposition from church, citizen groups and confounded city councils.","subtitle":"Laboratory of Fun and a University of the Streets","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/fun-palace.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/283-cedric-price-fun-palace","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.518722,-0.0007261,13z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":139.88,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":464.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":647.24,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":11,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":381,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":671.76,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1034.84,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1547.08,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":414.28,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1021.72,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1036.76,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1132.72,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":873.92,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1684.84,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":744.8,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":796.08,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":883.28,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":676.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1117.36,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1903.76,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":651.64,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":83.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":138.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":637.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":681.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1943.6,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1104.36,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"}]},"GLC":{"slug":"glc","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The Greater London Council (GLC) was the top-tier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_government\">local government</a> administrative body for <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_London\">Greater London</a> from 1965 to 1986. It replaced the earlier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_County_Council\">London County Council</a> (LCC) which had covered a much smaller area. The GLC was dissolved in 1986 by the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_Government_Act_1985\">Local Government Act 1985</a> and its powers were devolved to the London boroughs and other entities.</p>","subtitle":"Greater London Council","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/glc.jpg","external":"http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/march/31/newsid_2530000/2530803.stm","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":205.2,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":492.2,"topicId":"southbank","topicLabel":"SOUTHBANK"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":143,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1549.2,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"Hot Stuff Club":{"slug":"hot-stuff-club","body":"Price chose his company with care. He founded the Hot Stuff Club in 1970, but is reluctant to discuss its activities. Few architects have been granted membership of this exclusive cabal, characterised by its eccentric sartorial and dietary rules (members were given badges made from the caps of old-fashioned mustard pots and mustard was always on the table at meetings) and encouragement of witty conversation and wicked gossip. Price had architect friends, Norman Foster, for example, and former assistant Will Alsop, \"but we rarely talk about architecture\".","subtitle":"“Exclusive Cabal”","image":null,"external":"http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/4708428/Think-the-unimaginable.html","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":54.36,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1205.88,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":815.88,"topicId":"hot-stuff-club","topicLabel":"Hot Stuff Club"}]},"Housing research":{"slug":"housing-research","body":"Research into flexible, pre-fabricated, short life housing in the UK. Published in AD in 1971.","subtitle":"Cedric Price architects","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/housing-research.png","external":"http://www.aaschool.ac.uk/PUBLIC/AAPUBLICATIONS/aboutpublications.php","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/United+Kingdom/@55.378051,-3.435973,7z","also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1312.44,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"London Zoo Aviary":{"slug":"london-zoo-aviary","body":"The Aviary at London Zoo is one of the few built projects, realised in collaboration with the engineer Frank Newby and Lord Snowdon. This aviary has been a landmark on the London skyline since it was built in 1962. It was the first walk-through aviary in existence - pushing the architectural boundaries of its time. This aviary is home to some of the most majestic birds in the world, including white ibis and peacocks.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1176.4,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":404.24,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1366.32,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":662.72,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":713.16,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1719.8,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":982.08,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"}]},"National Federation of Demolition Contractors":{"slug":"national-federation-of-demolition-contractors","body":"The National Federation of Demolition Contractors (NFDC) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Construction\">construction</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_association\">trade association</a> representing companies involved in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demolition\">demolition</a> work.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\nThe NFDC represents its sector as one of four representative bodies on the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UK_Contractors_Group\">UK Contractors Group</a>.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1210.8,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"}]},"Oxford Corner House":{"slug":"oxford-corner-house","body":"Cedric Price’s Oxford Corner House (1965-66) was an unrealized project for a public communications and learning centre, modeled loosely on Price’s contemporary <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/283\">Fun Palace</a>. The project was to transform the physical interior and the conceptual operation of the J. Lyons &amp; Co. Corner House located on Oxford Street in central London<sup>1</sup>. The result was to be a 24-hour “information hive” with facilities for conferences, teaching, exhibitions, computer use, a planetarium, an audio/visual library, and public seating areas, for up to 5,000 people. An alternative to England’s small, exclusive universities, it would also function as a “town hall,” increasing the visibility and accessibility of educational facilities and information services within the urban core.","subtitle":"By Cedric Price architects","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/oxford-corner-house.png","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/1292-the-information-hive","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Oxford+St/@51.5154003,-0.1412821,14z","also":[{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":682.16,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1746.76,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"}]},"Potteries Thinkbelt housing types":{"slug":"potteries-thinkbelt-housing-types","body":"Four types of housing were designed: crate, battery, capsule and sprawl. All are designed by name to goad a profession hard-wired into rejecting such terms having any relation to the sacrosanct Vitruvian principles of ‘commodity, firmness and delight’.","subtitle":"Crate, battery, capsule and sprawl","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/potteries-thinkbelt-housing-types.jpg","external":"http://www.amazon.com/Cedric-Price-Potteries-Thinkbelt-SuperCrit/dp/0415434122","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Madeley,+Staffordshire,+UK/@52.997861,-2.339182,7z","also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":232.04,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":612.24,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1319.68,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"Potteries Thinkbelt":{"slug":"potteries-thinkbelt","body":"Price adopted the advancement of science and engineering working with industry as core content for his scheme – the integration of manufacturing and learning – but, took the challenge of changing attitudes towards education a step further. The opening statement of the publication of his project in New Society opens with: “Higher education and further education should become a major industrial undertaking, not a service run by gentlemen for the few.” His ‘Thinkbelt’ – the neologism alone asks that we free up preconceptions of the typology - goes beyond both the traditional monastic form of “polite cathedral town amenity” and the then current trend for building huge new campus’s on isolated greenfield sites.","subtitle":"A city caused by learning","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/potteries-thinkbelt.jpg","external":"https://vimeo.com/37940157\nhttp://www.supercrits.com/1/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Keele+University/@53.0084306,-2.2615387,14z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":140.88,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1118,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1573.96,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":553.72,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1173.44,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1504.76,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":2065.84,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1034.72,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1249.88,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1686.92,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":371.12,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1087.4,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1251.96,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":881.64,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":918.4,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":118.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":129.36,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":355.12,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"RIBA":{"slug":"riba","body":"The Royal Institute of British Architects (RIBA) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_body\">professional body</a> for architects primarily in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a>, but also internationally, founded for the advancement of architecture under its charter granted in 1837 and Supplemental Charter granted in 1971.","subtitle":"Royal Institute of British Architects","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/riba.jpg","external":"http://www.architecture.com","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=66+Portland+Pl,+London+W1B,+UK","also":[{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":460,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":625.52,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":742.16,"topicId":"appearance","topicLabel":"Appearance"}]},"Staffordshire Potteries":{"slug":"staffordshire-potteries","body":"The Staffordshire Potteries is a generic term for the industrial area encompassing the six towns (<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunstall,_Staffordshire\">Tunstall</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burslem\">Burslem</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanley,_Staffordshire\">Hanley</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoke-upon-Trent\">Stoke</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fenton,_Staffordshire\">Fenton</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longton,_Staffordshire\">Longton</a>) that now make up <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoke-on-Trent\">Stoke-on-Trent</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staffordshire\">Staffordshire</a>, England. The name is now synonymous with Stoke-on-Trent itself. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoke_City_FC\">Stoke City FC</a> are referred to as The Potters.","subtitle":"Industrial area","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/staffordshire-potteries.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staffordshire_Potteries","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=Staffordshire,+UK","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1256.4,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1048.6,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":923.16,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"Steel House competition":{"slug":"steel-house-competition","body":"Cedric Price designed the Steelhouse as a response to the increasing requirements for less definitive space. He argued that the main design criterion should be for the provision of space with maximum variation of possible uses. Features of the plan are: a shared activity area that is variable over a 24-hour cycle; alternative access routes (internal and external); capacity for subdivision into 2 homes; possibility of permanently fragmented ‘home’ with self-contained units and separate external access.","subtitle":"Cedric Price architects","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/steel-house-competition.jpg","external":"http://www.aaschool.ac.uk/PUBLIC/AAPUBLICATIONS/aboutpublications.php","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/United+Kingdom/@55.378051,-3.435973,7z","also":[{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1401.32,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]}}}